{:edb/db-before-t 2667 :edb/db-after-t 2668 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535980 50 #inst "2026-10-09T12:13:02.728Z" 13194139535980 true] [17592186152150 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378052 13194139535980 true] [17592186152151 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378059 13194139535980 true] [17592186152307 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535980 true] [17592186152307 1100 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-5I$ and $A$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized  eigenvector of rank $2$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ of $A$\n- $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of $A$\n- $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ of $A$" 13194139535980 true] [17592186152307 1100 "$u→^(A - 5 I)v→^(A - 5 I)w→^(A - 5 I)0; x→^(A)y→^(A)0$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A - 5I$ and $A$ on the nonzero vectors $u, v, w, x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 5$ of $A$\n2. $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 0$ of $A$\n3. $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 5$ of $A$" 13194139535980 false] [17592186152307 1145 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-5I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is an generalized eigenvector of rank $2$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A=A-0I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\nu\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-5I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3,$ not $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535980 true] [17592186152309 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535980 true] [17592186152309 1100 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$" 13194139535980 true] [17592186152309 1100 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}, v = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ =-5$ of $A$\n2. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ =-5$ of $A$\n3. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ =-5$ of $A$" 13194139535980 false] [17592186152309 1145 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+5I:$\n\n$$\nA+5I=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+5I:$\n\n$$\n(A+5I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+5I:$\n\n$$\n(A+5I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A+5I:$\n\n$$\n(A+5I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+5I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535980 true] [17592186378052 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378053 13194139535980 true] [17592186378052 1089 "q-109360" 13194139535980 true] [17592186378052 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535980 true] [17592186378052 1100 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+9I$ and $A-I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-9$ of $A$\n- $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n- $z$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$" 13194139535980 true] [17592186378052 1113 #uuid "9db1d36f-8952-504c-88e1-4f7cab566b72" 13194139535980 true] [17592186378052 1145 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false.  Since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n and $v\\ne 0,$ \ni.e. $A+9I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $1.$\n\n- Statement II is  false. Since \n\n$$\nx\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n \ni.e. $A-I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed, since \n\n$$\nz\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-1I$ maps $z$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535980 true] [17592186378053 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378054 13194139535980 true] [17592186378053 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378055 13194139535980 true] [17592186378053 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378056 13194139535980 true] [17592186378053 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378057 13194139535980 true] [17592186378053 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378058 13194139535980 true] [17592186378053 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535980 true] [17592186378053 1041 "selection" 13194139535980 true] [17592186378053 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378056 13194139535980 true] [17592186378053 1110 #uuid "d37f91b0-a1b7-5a7f-9c38-94732aeca611" 13194139535980 true] [17592186378054 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535980 true] [17592186378054 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535980 true] [17592186378054 1111 #uuid "48ddbdfb-1b66-576d-b4f0-9feeacfb4c33" 13194139535980 true] [17592186378055 1034 "I only" 13194139535980 true] [17592186378055 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535980 true] [17592186378055 1111 #uuid "bbf11992-3d37-5893-9112-521af3c02de5" 13194139535980 true] [17592186378056 1034 "III only" 13194139535980 true] [17592186378056 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535980 true] [17592186378056 1111 #uuid "c8479879-0436-5ada-a2f4-69dc54a1df99" 13194139535980 true] [17592186378057 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535980 true] [17592186378057 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535980 true] [17592186378057 1111 #uuid "a7e83f97-3c83-5670-bfde-c